  "notes": "<p><strong>Photo:</strong> The two marble statues known as Kleobis and Biton, about 580 BCE, found at Delphi. Archaeological Museum of Delphi. <em>Photo Credit: Photo by Joyofmuseums, CC BY-SA 4.0, via <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kleobis_and_Biton_-_Delphi_Archaeological_Museum_by_Joy_of_Museums.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikimedia Commons</a>.</em></p><p><strong>Based on evidence:</strong> At Delphi, people who came to consult the god paid a fee, had a first sacrifice made for them, entered the temple, and sacrificed again before the inner room. Then, accompanied by the male interpreters, they approached the Pythia, the god's priestess. She spoke, and the interpreters shaped her words into the answer, which was probably not in verse as Herodotus often reports it. Oracles in Herodotus are often obscure, and men often understand them too late. Herodotus wrote that Kleobis and Biton, two young men of Argos, pulled their mother's wagon to the festival of Hera, fell asleep in the sanctuary and never woke again, and that the Argives made statues of them and dedicated them at Delphi.</p><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li>Herodotus, <em>Histories</em> 1.31.2 to 5 (written in the later 400s BCE), in <em>The Landmark Herodotus</em>, edited by Robert B. Strassler.</li><li>\"Oracles, Religion, and Politics in Herodotus\", Appendix P in <em>The Landmark Herodotus</em>, edited by Robert B. Strassler, §8 and §9.</li><li>Statues of Kleobis and Biton, Archaeological Museum of Delphi, as recorded on Wikimedia Commons.</li></ul>"
